# Mutation cases for what the `lua` selection of SCE_SCRIPT_ENGINE reads.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/lua_engine_reads_ecmascript.cases`.
#
# `SCE_SCRIPT_ENGINE=lua` is a listed, validated value of a cache entry. The
# engine it selects is Lua 5.4 with `sce-build`'s ECMAScript frontend lowering
# the author's ECMAScript at run time, so `EcmaScriptSemanticsOnLuaEngine` names
# that engine rather than asking the provider — otherwise the seam would be
# compiled by every build and measured by none.
#
# ⚠ This header used to add "and no gate configures it". That stopped being
# true on 2026-08-29: `scripts/gate ecma262-lowered-cpp` configures a throwaway
# `-DSCE_SCRIPT_ENGINE=lua` tree and runs `LoweredEcma262` in it. That suite is
# NOT reachable from this casefile — `mutation_ctest` drives `--test-dir build`,
# the developer's quickjs tree, where `LoweredEcma262` does not exist. So the
# gates that lane added are witnessed by hand rather than by this corpus, and
# that is an open residue, not a claim that they are unmutated.
#
# Measured 2026-08-18 against the shared ECMA-262 table, that engine answered
# 44 of 98 expressions differently from the language. Twenty-one of those were
# not divergences of meaning at all: they were shared runtime definitions the
# transformer never named, and text it emitted that is not Lua. Each case below
# restores one of them. The remaining 23 were declared, case by case, in
# `tests/ecmascript/lua_engine_divergences.json`, and the last two cases here
# are what holds that file to both directions.
#
# ⚠ That 23 is now 12, and the change is what made one of those two cases go
# stale. On 2026-08-29 `LuaEngine::loweredTextOf` began offering the author's
# ECMAScript to `sce-build`'s frontend PARSER against an EMPTY scope, so every
# CLOSED expression is answered there and never reaches the rewriter. Eleven
# entries left the file, `5 ^ 3` among them, and the case anchored on it
# matched nothing — which reads as SURVIVED. It is re-aimed at `++v`, which
# names a variable and so is still the rewriter's. The seam that did this has
# a case of its own below: it is now the engine's first answer, and a corpus
# that mutates only the rewriter would stop covering the path most expressions
# take.
#
# ⚠⚠ Run in full on 2026-08-29 rather than reasoned about, and THREE cases came
# back SURVIVED for exactly that reason — every one of them broke the rewriter
# under a CLOSED expression the frontend now answers first. The measurement is
# the lesson: a seam that takes work away from a component takes the corpus's
# grip on it at the same moment, silently, and only a full round says so. One
# was re-aimed at an open expression, two followed their subject to the
# frontend, and each is recorded where it happened rather than here.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^(ECMAScriptSemantics)$'
mutation_targets sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp
# Read at run time rather than compiled in: the declared list is a fixture the
# test opens by the path CMake injects.
mutation_runtime_targets tests/ecmascript/lua_engine_divergences.json
# The suite each case reds, measured: `EcmaScriptSemantics` and
# `EcmaScriptSemanticsOnLuaEngine` are the two test suites in the one ctest
# test this selector names.
mutation_oracles tests/engine/EcmaScriptSemanticsTest.cpp

# ── Five cases left this file with the rewriter ─────────────────────
#
# `sce/src/scripting/EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer.cpp` was a target here and five
# cases broke it: the string-literal placeholder that made a literal invisible
# as a receiver, the shared-definition map, `concat`'s fold, `push`'s length,
# and the index base. On 2026-08-29 the rewriter was RETIRED — nothing in the
# engine reaches it, which the seam document's `retire-rewriter` row asserts and
# `the_rewriter_is_retired_not_merely_second.cases` mutates — so breaking it can
# no longer change what this suite sees, and all five would have reported
# SURVIVED.
#
# Four followed their subject to `sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs` and live in
# `the_frontend_now_owns_the_member_surface.cases`, judged by the frontend's own
# golden over the same shared table. The fifth is retired rather than moved: a
# placeholder that defeats a backward scan is a defect a PARSER cannot have, so
# there is no frontend mutation to aim at it, and inventing one would be a case
# about nothing.
#
# ⚠ That is the third time in one day that this file has lost cases the same
# way, and the count is the point. The header above already names the mechanism
# — a seam takes the corpus's grip at the moment it takes the work — and the
# retirement is that mechanism running to completion. Nothing here was weakened;
# the code each case named stopped being on any path.

# ── Rounding and unicode escapes moved with their owner ─────────────
#
# Two cases stood here — `Math.round` rounding the language's way §15-8-2-15,
# and a `\uXXXX` escape reaching Lua in a spelling it can read §12-9-4 — and on
# 2026-08-29 both reported SURVIVED. Neither mutation had stopped being a
# defect; both had stopped being OBSERVABLE from this suite. The shared table
# asks `Math.round(2.5)` and `'é'` as CLOSED expressions, the seam below
# answers every closed expression in `sce-build`'s frontend, and the rewriter
# code these cases break is no longer what the engine's answer depends on for
# either one.
#
# They were not deleted and they were not re-aimed at some other expression:
# they followed their subject to the code that now owns it, and live in
# `the_frontend_now_owns_rounding_and_escapes.cases` against the frontend's own
# suite. That is the shape this corpus should take as the rewriter retires —
# a case moves when the behaviour moves, so coverage tracks the seam instead of
# thinning out behind it.
#
# ⚠ What is NOT covered either way: the rewriter's own `Math.round` and escape
# handling is still live for an OPEN expression, and the shared table samples
# neither feature open. Closing that means adding open cases to
# `tests/ecmascript/ecma262_semantics.json`, which every backend reads — an
# expansion with its own lanes to answer, not a line to slip into this round.

# ── The declared list is held to BOTH directions ────────────────────
#
# A one-sided verdict rots: the count reached 44 while a comment said 26
# precisely because nothing asked the second question. The two directions are
#
#   (a) an observed disagreement that is NOT on the list, and
#   (b) an entry on the list that no longer disagrees.
#
# ⚠ There used to be two cases here, one per direction, and BOTH edited the
# list. On 2026-08-29 the list reached ZERO and the (a) case went stale in the
# most literal way available: its mutation was "remove a live entry", and there
# was no entry left to remove. An empty list cannot witness (a) by being
# edited, and a case that cannot apply reads as SURVIVED rather than as absent.
#
# It is not replaced by another list edit, because (a) is now carried by the
# two SEAM cases below: each of them makes the engine really disagree with the
# shared table while this list stays empty, which is the (a) failure in its
# natural form rather than a simulation of it. That is the same principle the
# rounding and escape cases followed out of this file — a case moves to
# whatever now owns its question, and only (b) is still a question about the
# list's own text.

mutation_case "a repaired divergence stays on the declared list" <<'PY'
edit("tests/ecmascript/lua_engine_divergences.json",
     """  "divergences": []""",
     """  "divergences": [
    {
      "source": "!a",
      "clause": "12.5.9 logical NOT",
      "needs": "operand-boundaries",
      "diverges_on": ["runtime-rewriter"]
    }
  ]""")
PY

# ── The seam offers expressions to the frontend at all ──────────────
#
# `LuaEngine::loweredTextOf` asks the frontend, and since the rewriter was
# retired that is the ONLY thing it asks: deleting the call leaves the engine
# refusing every ECMAScript expression rather than answering it worse. Doing so
# while leaving the paragraph that explains it standing is the shape a careless
# revert has.
#
# ⚠ The mutation changed with the retirement, and the direction it changed is
# worth keeping. It used to put the whole shared table back on the rewriter —
# `1 == '1'` stopped coercing, `-7 % 3` stopped truncating — so the suite went
# red on WRONG ANSWERS. Now it goes red on refusals. Both are red; only the
# second one is a failure a person reading the output cannot mistake for a
# semantics bug, which is the trade this seam was retired for.
#
# ⚠ `(void)scope;` is what keeps the mutation COMPILING. Without it the
# parameter is unused, `-Werror` refuses the build, and a mutation the compiler
# refused proves nothing either way. It used to be `(void)closedLoweringScope()`
# — the scope was a process-wide constant then, and the case had to be re-aimed
# when it became a session's argument.
#
# ⚠⚠ This case also stands in for a check that could not see it. The D1 ledger's
# `decision:linked-beside-lua` row asserts the engine still calls the surface,
# and until 2026-08-29 it searched one whole FILE — so this exact mutation left
# the ledger green while the suite went red. The row now reads code with
# commentary cut away, across every source under `sce/src/scripting/`; this case
# is the witness that keeps saying so.

mutation_case "the seam stops offering expressions to the frontend" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp",
     "    return scope.lowerValue(expression.text());",
     """    (void)scope;
    return std::nullopt;""")
PY

# ── The script path offers STATEMENTS to the frontend ───────────────
#
# The sibling of the case above, one grammar up, and it is the one that took
# this repository's divergence list to zero. `EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer::
# transformScript` replaced text without a parse, so a `continue` became
# `_ = continue` and the loop it belonged to stopped skipping an iteration — a
# defect no scope can reach, because a scope only decides which NAMES resolve.
# `LuaEngine::loweredScriptOf` asks `sce_lower_script`, and the shared table's
# `while`/`continue` case is what turns red when it stops.
#
# ⚠ `(void)scope;` again, and for the same reason: without it the parameter is
# unused and `-Werror` refuses the mutated build, which would report this as
# INCONCLUSIVE rather than as evidence either way.

mutation_case "the script path stops offering statements to the frontend" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp",
     "    return scope.lowerScript(script.text());",
     """    (void)scope;
    return std::nullopt;""")
PY
